Docker Container Lifecycle and Image in Linux
===============================================================
Goal: Install the container tool, search/pull the nginx image, run
a container named 'webserver' on port 8080, then stop, restart,
remove the container and the image, and verify nothing is left.

NOTE: In your screenshots the tool used is PODMAN (CentOS Stream 9
ships podman, not docker). Podman commands are the same as Docker
commands. If your lab uses real Docker, just replace "podman" with
"docker" in every command below.


STEP 1: Check installation and running status
---------------------------------------------
Install (if not installed):
$ sudo dnf install podman -y

Check it is installed:
$ podman --version
$ rpm -q podman

(Podman has no background service, so there is nothing to start.)

If you are using real Docker instead:
$ sudo systemctl status docker
$ sudo systemctl start docker


STEP 2: Display the version
---------------------------
$ podman -v

Example output: podman version 3.4.3-dev


STEP 3: Search for the official nginx image
-------------------------------------------
$ podman search nginx

Look for the row:
docker.io/library/nginx   Official build of Nginx.   [OK]

Optional (show only official images):
$ podman search nginx --filter=is-official


STEP 4: Pull the latest nginx image and verify
----------------------------------------------
$ podman pull docker.io/library/nginx

Verify it downloaded:
$ podman images

Expected: docker.io/library/nginx   latest   <IMAGE ID>   <size>


STEP 5: Start container 'webserver' (detached, port 80 -> 8080)
---------------------------------------------------------------
$ podman run -d -p 8080:80 --name webserver docker.io/library/nginx

(-d = detached/background, -p 8080:80 = host port 8080 -> container
 port 80, --name = container name)

It prints a long container ID.


STEP 6: Verify the container is running and web page works
----------------------------------------------------------
$ podman ps

Check: STATUS "Up ...", PORTS 0.0.0.0:8080->80/tcp, NAMES webserver

Test the web page:
$ curl http://localhost:8080
(or open http://localhost:8080 in Firefox)

Expected: "Welcome to nginx!" page


STEP 7: Stop the running container and verify it is stopped
-----------------------------------------------------------
$ podman stop webserver
$ podman ps
(empty list = nothing running)
$ podman ps -a
(webserver shows STATUS "Exited")


STEP 8: Restart the container and verify it is running again
------------------------------------------------------------
$ podman start webserver
$ podman ps

Or restart directly:
$ podman restart webserver

IMPORTANT: Use "podman start webserver" (the existing container).
Do NOT use "podman run" again, because that creates a NEW container
with a random name (like frosty_leavitt in your screenshot).


STEP 9: Stop and remove the 'webserver' container
-------------------------------------------------
$ podman stop webserver
$ podman rm webserver

Shortcut (force stop + remove):
$ podman rm -f webserver


STEP 10: Remove the nginx image
-------------------------------
$ podman rmi docker.io/library/nginx

(The image cannot be removed while a container still uses it. If
 you accidentally created extra containers, remove them first with
 podman ps -a and podman rm <name>.)


STEP 11: Verify no nginx containers or images remain
----------------------------------------------------
$ podman ps -a
$ podman images

Both lists should be empty (only headers shown).


QUICK COMMAND SUMMARY
---------------------
sudo dnf install podman -y                          -> install
podman --version                                    -> check install
podman -v                                           -> version
podman search nginx                                 -> search image
podman pull docker.io/library/nginx                 -> download image
podman images                                       -> list images
podman run -d -p 8080:80 --name webserver docker.io/library/nginx
podman ps                                           -> running containers
curl http://localhost:8080                          -> test web page
podman stop webserver                               -> stop
podman ps -a                                        -> all containers
podman start webserver                              -> start again
podman rm -f webserver                              -> remove container
podman rmi docker.io/library/nginx                  -> remove image
podman ps -a ; podman images                        -> verify clean
